Algorithm:

Step 1: Create an empty list to store heap elements.
Step 2: Insert the first element of the array into the list as the root of the heap.
Step 3: Traverse the remaining elements of the array one by one.
Step 4: Insert the current element at the end of the heap.
Step 5: Treat the inserted element as the current node.
Step 6: Find the parent position of the current node.
Step 7: Compare the current node value with its parent value.
Step 8: If the current node is greater than the parent, swap their positions.
Step 9: Move the current node to the parent position and repeat the comparison.
Step 10: Continue this process until the Max Heap property is satisfied or the root is reached.
Step 11: Repeat the same procedure for all elements of the array.
Step 12: After all insertions are completed, the list represents a valid Max Heap.

Time Complexity: O(n log n)
Space Complexity: O(n)


-----> Code <-----

import java.util.*;
class heap{
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc=new Scanner(System.in);
        int arr[]={30, 50, 10, 40, 20, 5, 1, 35, 2};
        ArrayList<Integer> al=new ArrayList<>();
        al.add(arr[0]);
        for(int i=1;i<arr.length;i++){
            al.add(arr[i]);
            int curr=al.size()-1;
            while(curr>0){
                int parent=(curr-1)/2;
                if(al.get(curr)>al.get(parent)){
                    int temp=al.get(curr);
                    al.set(curr,al.get(parent));
                    al.set(parent,temp);

                    curr=parent;
                }
                else break;
            }
        }
        System.err.println(al);
    }
}
